    "media:imagetext": "lawyer - then back to Wiesbaden for the night with a letter of agreement dram up.\nThursday morning we signed the agreement and that afternoon I caught the PAA Clipper\nto London. Sorry to be so cagey about my business deal but it is still \"restricted\"\nas the Army says. However, it turned out better than I had hoped and quite an im\nprovement over what I had been authorized to sign, so feel all right about it •\nStopped off in London for a day to see our people there and left Friday\nnight for New York. There were storms out over our regular track across the North\nAtlantic, so we were routed via Iceland. That was really a break for me because\nIceland was new territory - in fact about the only country I haven't visited before.\nWe flew over the capital city of Reykjavik in bright moonlight and it was almost 11ke\ndaylight. Landed at the Keflavik Airport in strange and unexpected contrast with a\nsnow-covered Idlewild Airport at New Iork where me took off between banks of snow on\neither side of the runway; the fields of Europe were as green as mid-gummer and even\nin Iceland there was no snow. Furthermore, it was a lot warmer than New Iork. We re-\nfueled and then flew direct to New York. As in the case of my flight over, it was as\nsmooth as glass except over the tip of Greenland where we ran into a little turbulence.\nArrived in New York one minute ahead of schedule.\nThe Germans I talked to were mostly industrialists and bankers. None thought\nthat aything would come of the Four Power discussions in Berlin. They all agreed that\n1f a really free election was held in East Germany the Communists wouldn't get enough\nvotes to fill a hat. In these circumstances the Russians are not likely to approve\nany election except one of those Red \"free elections\" where they get all the votes.\nHowever, It must be remembered that the Russians are unpredictable. In the recent\nWest German election the voters were as free as in the U.S. and the Commies failed to\nwin a single contest. The Germans with whom I talked were not too keen on a reunion\nwith East Germany. The West Germans have done a magnificent job of reconstruction while\nthe Russians in East Germany have done absolutely nothing. So if there mere a reunion\nthe demands of East Germany on the economy, the need for reconstruction and new\ncapital would set the whole economy back several years. It might lead to severe\ninflation.\n- 3 -",
